#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222357} :activity/id #uuid "d3cfa4d0-9bee-52e4-bb9e-39444a417cfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222476 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "44078a68-8d27-5f6e-adec-348668ecef51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14326 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "23a4eff8-b2bf-51f0-87ea-b1466d7af3ca" :step/math-academy-id 600094 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222509 :tutorial/content "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "511f0e8e-f19d-5e1e-955e-97dbd8386ea2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14590 :tutorial/title "Using the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3390cec0-8a87-5c98-86f2-ba843c736c0d" :step/math-academy-id 600095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221155 :question/id #uuid "fe6b394f-a130-5746-a5c9-0fbbee020554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048865}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441320}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fc54e4ac-72ee-57e2-8339-74eb6e586087" :step/math-academy-id 600096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222360}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221167 :question/id #uuid "05394517-ee39-5b77-9f84-b439d907dd3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445925}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"} :step/id #uuid "58c13b52-ac11-56f0-9289-23be6f240ad2" :step/math-academy-id 600100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222508 :tutorial/content "Recall that the first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nThis means the new Laplace transform is the original graph of $F$ evaluated at $s-a$.\n\nSo, the graph shift is:\n\n- If $a>0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the right** by $a$ units.\n\n- If $a<0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the left** by $|a|$ units.\n\nFor example, suppose $F(s)$ has the following graph:\n\n![](images/45/45ce62c981e4c7853ef158046eb21fbc9c135f5fa0a8887d813a07594077c8ce.png)\n\nThen the Laplace transform of $e^{-3t} f(t)$ is $F(s+3).$ Here, $a=-3$, so this corresponds to shifting the graph of $F(s)$ three units to the left:\n\n![](images/b7/b75658824079fe90c9c4f43d4d37d355c95b845ebed3f11bdf751b52f8d34f55.png)\n\nA useful self-check is that any horizontal features move the same way:\n\n- vertical asymptotes shift left/right by the same amount,\n\n- intercepts and key points shift left/right by the same amount." :tutorial/id #uuid "db14b380-2e8e-5007-a9ad-722a83b574ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14589 :tutorial/title "Graph Shifting"} :step/id #uuid "c7227d83-b5a7-5418-8e1d-915b257ae283" :step/math-academy-id 600103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221162 :question/id #uuid "1e5e6e3a-306b-5e8c-a8ba-baa0d63a1813" :question/math-academy-id "e-21549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048419}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445926}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "b89a14ea-72ce-5ea2-90f1-e3c54fe52d78" :step/math-academy-id 600104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221159 :question/id #uuid "04233a8a-069f-5649-b198-c6bbf5b96742" :question/math-academy-id "e-21531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048865}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441321}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving the First Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3f432000-4480-52b3-b24b-72186edeb9f6" :step/math-academy-id 600108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222364}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222520 :tutorial/content "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Compare With |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $e^{at} t^n$ | $\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$ | $\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbf3c06b-f0f4-528b-aff6-5b6adcae65b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14692 :tutorial/title "A Laplace Transforms Comparison Table"} :step/id #uuid "aa6dc187-37cc-58ad-bd19-cba1f304979e" :step/math-academy-id 600111}] :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221462}]}
